  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 AM CST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nToday - Sunday: Light Precipitation Chances\n\nA weak front continues to make its way across the Upper Midwest. \nJust enough lift is combining with PWATs in the 0.4 to 0.6\" range to \nproduce some light precipitation. Not much is expected as far \namounts go. This is supported by earlier observations across central \nMinnesota to northwest Wisconsin where only a trace of precipitation \nwas recorded. While CAMs have precipitation increasing in coverage \nover the next few hours as well as amounts (generally less than \n0.03\"), the main question is whether or not more precipitation can \nreach the ground rather than a few sprinkles or flurries here and \nthere. Similar to yesterdays forecast, forecast soundings show a dry \nsub-cloud layer in the lowest 5000ft for areas south of I-94. Unless \nthe atmosphere can saturate more in the lower levels, would not \nexpect anything more than a few light showers or flurries for these \nlocations. The biggest question for early this morning is if \nfreezing rain/drizzle is going to occur. Confidence is still quite \nlow on this occurring (10%) as even looking at a sounding from KMDZ, \nthe dry sub-cloud layer is not as stout as locations further to the \nsouth, but there remains little lift in the column. Despite this, \nareas north of I-94 would have the best chance at seeing freezing \nrain/drizzle with temperatures hovering near freezing. Since \nconfidence is still very low in this occurring, have continued the \ntrend of removing freezing rain from the forecast and instead \nreplacing it with snow (or rain/snow showers) as this would seem \nmore likely to occur. Regardless of the precipitation type, minimal \naccumulation is likely. \n\nAfter the front moves through during the morning, the region remains \nin west to southwest flow which helps warm up temperatures into the \nmid 40s to low 50s. Heading into tonight, the models continue to \nshow a shortwave phasing with another wave in the central Plains \nregion. The track of the associated surface low remains consistent \namongst model consensus to stay well south of the forecast area. \nBoth ML and AI guidance have maintained their trends that the bulk \nof precipitation stays more in the mid Mississippi River Valley. \nThere still remains a 30 to 60% chance for precipitation to occur \nfor areas south of I-90 as recent CAM runs have shown precipitation \nreaching into portions of northeast Iowa and southwest Wisconsin.\n\nMonday - Thursday: Mild and Dry\n\nOn Monday and Tuesday, the 500 mb pattern will be mainly zonal \nacross the northern US and southern Canada. As a result, \ntemperatures will be near to slightly above normal (in the 30s).\nOn Tuesday night, a trough will move into the southwest US and then \nit moves east into the Southern and Central Plains on Wednesday \nnight and Thursday. The winds aloft will become more southerly. High \ntemperatures will range from the mid-40s to mid-50s. These \ntemperatures are more typical of late October and early November. \nDuring this stretch, dry conditions are expected as the majority of \nthe precipitation stays in southern Canada and the southern\nPlains.",
